  [== Line drivers
  Line drivers are specialized amplifiers used to improve the signal integrity and driving capability of an analog or digital signal at its source. They act as impedance matching buffers, driving the transmission line with a higher-than-normal current or voltage level to overcome the parasitic capacitance and signal attenuation inherent to long cables.

  #align(center, image("../images/50/LD.png", width: 100%))

  In this example, the `MAX232` line driver acts as a transceiver to convert signals from single-ended TTL logic levels (0 to 5V) to the robust RS-232 standard levels (-9V to +9V), significantly increasing noise immunity over longer cable runs.], 

  [== Differential signaling
  Differential signaling transmits information using two complementary electrical signals sent on paired wires. The receiving circuit (a differential amplifier) extracts the data by measuring the voltage difference between the inverting and non-inverting inputs, rather than referencing a single wire to absolute ground.
  
  This technique is highly robust and best suited for high-speed protocols like RS-485, CAN bus, and USB.

  #align(center, image("../images/50/DS.png", width: 90%))
  #align(center, image("../images/50/differential_signaling.png", width: 90%))]
)

== Simplex - Half-Duplex - Duplex communication
#grid(
  columns: (1fr, 1fr),
  gutter: 3em,

  [#align(horizon, image("../images/50/S-D-FD.png", width: 90%))],

  [
  (a) A *simplex* communication system transmits data in only one direction. No return communication is possible on that channel (e.g., basic sensor telemetry or standard radio broadcasts).

  (b) A *full-duplex* system allows bidirectional communication to occur simultaneously, typically requiring separate transmission and reception channels.

  (c) A *half-duplex* system provides bidirectional communication only in one direction at a time. Devices must take turns transmitting and receiving over a shared medium, requiring a mechanism for collision avoidance and medium access control.]
)

