== Synchronous and Asynchronous communication
#table(
  columns: (1fr, 1fr),
  gutter: 3em,

  [ #align(center, [*Synchronous communication*])
    #align(center, image("../images/50/sync.png", width: 80%))
    
    In synchronous protocols (e.g., SPI, I2C), the exact moment optimal for sampling the data line is dictated by a shared, continuous external clock signal generated by the bus master. This ensures tight temporal alignment between sender and receiver, allowing for high throughput. However, this comes at the cost of routing an extra dedicated trace/cable solely for the clock, which can introduce clock skew over long distances.],

  [ #align(center, [*Asynchronous communication*])
    #align(center, image("../images/50/async.png", width: 80%))
    
    Asynchronous communication (e.g., UART) lacks a shared clock. Instead, both devices must pre-agree on a specific data rate (Baud rate). The transmission begins with a *Start Bit* (a first transition from IDLE to LOW), which triggers an interrupt to synchronize the receiver's internal sampling clock for that specific data frame (*byte-wise sync*).

    The major downside is overhead: a non-negligible portion of the bandwidth is consumed by synchronization metadata (Start, Stop, and Parity bits). For a standard 8N1 UART configuration, 20% of the transmitted bits carry no payload data.]
)

