== Interrupt Event Selection 

The timing of when the CPU is notified via an Interrupt Flag (IF) is highly configurable. This allows the software to optimize CPU load.

_From the datasheet (Example for `UTXISEL`):_
```
bit 15,13 UTXISEL<1:0>: UARTx Transmission Interrupt Mode Selection bits
  11 = Reserved; do not use
  10 = Interrupt when a character is transferred to the Transmit Shift Register (TSR), 
       and as a result, the transmit buffer becomes completely empty.
  01 = Interrupt when the last character is shifted out of the Transmit Shift Register; 
       all transmit operations are fully completed (idle bus).
  00 = Interrupt when a character is transferred to the Transmit Shift Register 
       (implies there is at least one open slot in the transmit buffer).

bit 14    UTXINV: UARTx Transmit Polarity Inversion bit
  If IREN = 0 (Standard UART):
    1 = UxTX Idle state is ‘0’ (Low)
    0 = UxTX Idle state is ‘1’ (High - Standard default)
```
By modifying `UTXISEL` and `URXISEL`, you dictate the *condition* under which `UxTXIF` and `UxRXIF` are set to `1`. For example, setting `UTXISEL = 10` is useful if you want to refill the FIFO only when it has entirely run out of data, minimizing interrupt overhead.

#redBox([*Attention:*\
`UTXISEL` and `URXISEL` dictate *when* the hardware flag is raised, but they *do not enable the interrupt itself*! To allow the CPU to jump to the Interrupt Service Routine (ISR), you must explicitly set the corresponding Enable bits (e.g., `UxTXIE` and `UxRXIE`) in the core `IECx` (Interrupt Enable Control) registers.\

This two-step process (setting the condition with `UTXISEL`/`URXISEL` and enabling the interrupt in `IECx`) is a common source of confusion for beginners, as it can lead to situations where the flag is correctly set by the hardware but the CPU never responds because the interrupt was not enabled.])

